1. 01_data_preparation
    * Extract household data from Low Carbon London dataset
    * Perform initial filtering to remove variable tariff households 
    * Remove duplicates and invalid entries
    * Retain households with data coverage of more than 99% during the fixed study window
    * Retain households covering the required study period

2. 02_household_selection
    * Apply filters to filter out implausible or inactive households
    * Filters include:
        * Minimum standard deviation
        * Maximum half-hourly demand threshold
        * Maximum zero-reading ratio
    * Carry out manual quality check to remove households which passed filters but still remain implausible
    * Finalise selected household cohort

3. 03_feature_engineering
    * Extract weather data for study period
    * Perform cleaning, remove duplicates and forward filling missing values
    * Forward fill missing demand data 
    * Aggregate demand data to hourly
    * Merge weather and demand data
    * Create input features including:
        * Household demand
        * Temperature
        * Relative Humidity
        * time of day sin/cos encodings
        * day of week sin/cos encodings
        * time of year sin/cos encodings
    * Split datatset into train, val, test split chronologically to prevent data leakage
    * Perform min-max scaling using training data only
    * Demand scaling performed per household, allowing models to focus on shape rather than magnitude 

4. 04_centralised
    * Centralised screening + model shortlisting using pooled household data
    * Use Fixed seed used to allow for fair comparison
    * Centralised + Fine tuning testing 
    * Models tested:
        * CNN-LSTM hybrid models + Fully connected layer head variants
        * LSTM models + Fully connected layer head variants
        * Encoder Decoder model
        * Non recurrent dense/MLP baseline

5. 05_federated
    * Carry forward shortlisted models from centralised screening
    * Train models using Federated learning only
    * Train models using Federated learning + fine-tuning
    * Use FedAvg algorithm
    * Used 200 communication rounds and fit fraction of 0.3 to manage computational cost
    * Compare performance using validation set RMSE and MAE

6. 06_localised
    * Trained one model per household using shortlisted models 
    * Persistence used as a naive baseline
    * SARIMA used as a traditional household-level forecasting benchmark
    * Compare performance using validation set RMSE and MAE
  

7. 07_extension
    * Carry out extension studies on selected LSTM64_32 model
    * Performed K-means clustering on average daily load profiles of each household
    * Trained one FL model per cluster to determine if clustered FL helps performance
    * Tested extended communication rouinds to check if FL performance further improves beyond inital 200-round budget

8. 08_blind_house_test
    * Sampled 10 random households from eligible households which weren't in the main 100-household training cohort
    * Used limited data to test if FL + FT with LSTM64_32 model still retained its advantage over SARIMA 
    * Limited data split:
        * 5 days training
        * 2 days validation
        * 7 days test
    * Comparing:
        * Pretrained FL model
        * Pretrained FL model + local fine-tuning
        * SARIMA
    * Use test to see if selected FL+ fine-tuning pipeline adapts to unseen households with limited data

